WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.783
Vulnerability management fails at three
points assessors probe: a stale inventory,

2
00:00:05.783 --> 00:00:10.288
prioritization by score alone, and
no record of found, due and fixed.

3
00:00:10.288 --> 00:00:12.637
The program's answer is a daily queue.

4
00:00:12.637 --> 00:00:15.224
Pull the KEV feed before
the morning review.

5
00:00:15.224 --> 00:00:18.200
Pull findings from Defender
Vulnerability Management.

6
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.975
Join each device to the authoritative
asset record and its exposure tag.

7
00:00:22.975 --> 00:00:27.590
Look up CISA's automation and impact
data, apply the directive's table,

8
00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:31.012
and keep the first-detected
date, never resetting it.

9
00:00:31.012 --> 00:00:32.771
Use the catalog's due date,

10
00:00:32.771 --> 00:00:36.874
because Defender's vulnerability
data carries no KEV flag.

11
00:00:36.874 --> 00:00:40.850
Treat an asset with no exposure
tag as publicly exposed.

12
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:45.387
And Defender's exports carry no
history, so the program keeps its own.

13
00:00:45.387 --> 00:00:48.625
A scanner authors no
state; it measures a gap.

14
00:00:48.625 --> 00:00:51.746
Each platform patches with
its own native stack,

15
00:00:51.746 --> 00:00:56.662
and every stack returns the same
remediation record, keyed to the same asset.

16
00:00:56.662 --> 00:01:01.370
A finding closes when the fix is recorded
and the next scan no longer sees it.
